{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788686255781,"sessionID":"ses_f89fe1261ffe8viTNFDooEJYor","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-b3b7c4bed7c3d475","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-benchmark-fannkuch-redux-build/plans/board.yaml","offset":160,"limit":50},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-benchmark-fannkuch-redux-build/plans/board.yaml</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n160: - id: float-build-jq\n161:   issue: gh:149\n162:   title: 'Landed directly (2026-09-04), not by a delegated worker: full Float for jq -- lane had 4.3h-idle real progress (arithmetic, comparisons, input, a careful ESCALATION.md) stalled by the worker''s own model repeatedly corrupting a probe script with a stray Unicode combining character. Closed the escalation''s printing gap: jq''s tostring already gets the shortest round-trip digits right (verified against a ~5000-value fuzz run, matching float-build-c-escalation''s own verification), so its output is reparsed and relaid-out with the same notation rule runtime/toylang.c''s Native formatter uses, rather than needing a fresh Q37 decision round. One gap left open on purpose: a non-finite float nested in a Vec/Record diverges from JS (pinned by a test, not silently untested) -- fixing it means jq abandoning its value-based JSON encoder for hand-built string concatenation, a bigger change than this row''s scope.'\n163:   kind: build\n164:   needs: [float-build]\n165:   status: done\n166: \n167: - id: float-build-c-escalation\n168:   issue: gh:149\n169:   title: 'Landed directly (2026-09-04), not by a delegated worker: full Float support for Native/LLVM, not an escalation -- lane had sat delegated 23.5 hours, 0 commits, last run burned its whole budget re-deriving history before a blocked CLI call ended it. libc has no shortest-round-trip float formatter (the actual \"usable native matching primitive\" question), so one was built: increasing-precision search over %e for the digits, ECMA-262 Number::toString layout for the notation, verified against Node byte-for-byte across a ~5000-value fuzz run before landing in runtime/toylang.c. Also fixed a real Kind-mismatch bug in check/mod.rs''s `-x` desugaring (Float-typed zero built as Kind::Int(0)), general to every backend, that JS''s untyped runtime numbers were masking. Float still needs Go/Python/Rust/Lua before it can be a corpus case.'\n170:   kind: build\n171:   needs: [float-build]\n172:   status: done\n173: \n174: - id: benchmark-suite-build\n175:   title: 'Landed directly (2026-09-03), not by a delegated worker: the hyperfine harness (src/bin/bench.rs, just bench NAME) and binary-trees, the first of the 8 non-float CLBG tasks, per benchmark-plan.md''s Build status section. That section also corrects the suite spike''s \"good fit today\" claim: reverse-complement/k-nucleotide/regex-redux need Str slicing or Char -> Str, neither of which exists; pidigits needs a bignum type; mandelbrot needs float support most backends lack. fasta and fannkuch-redux are the two next real candidates (benchmark-fasta-build, benchmark-fannkuch-redux-build below); n-body/spectral-norm stay deferred (gh:146 ruling).'\n176:   kind: build\n177:   needs: []\n178:   prio: 4\n179:   status: done\n180: \n181: - id: benchmark-fasta-build\n182:   title: 'Build the CLBG fasta task in toylang (benchmark-plan.md Build status): generates DNA sequences from a small literal Str alphabet via a computed PRNG index, so it needs no Char -> Str conversion. Follows the pattern in benches/programs/binary-trees.toy -- program under benches/programs/, correctness pinned as a small-N tests/corpus/ case, a larger benches/inputs/ fixture for real timing.'\n183:   kind: build\n184:   needs: []\n185:   prio: 4\n186:   status: todo\n187: \n188: - id: benchmark-fannkuch-redux-build\n189:   title: 'Build the CLBG fannkuch-redux task in toylang (benchmark-plan.md Build status): permutations of Vec<Int>, fully numeric, no string decode needed. Follows the pattern in benches/programs/binary-trees.toy.'\n190:   kind: build\n191:   needs: []\n192:   prio: 4\n193:   status: todo\n194: \n195: - id: search-layer-build\n196:   title: Wire first/any/all into the general nondeterministic-evaluation model from draft.md#query-is-search (gh:142 ruling, coordinate with search-and-fold-design)\n197:   kind: build\n198:   needs: []\n199:   status: delegated\n200: \n201: - id: sort-by-max-by\n202:   issue: gh:177\n203:   title: sort_by and max_by -- ordering through a scalar projection (euler-ergonomics row 7)\n204:   kind: build\n205:   needs: [sum-max-builtins-build]\n206:   status: delegated\n207: \n208: - id: param-destructure-build\n209:   title: Implement match-arm-style parameter destructuring, e.g. fn gcd({a, b}:{a:Int,b:Int}) (gh:144 ruling)\n\n(Showing lines 160-209 of 488. Use offset=210 to continue.)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"- id: float-build-jq\n  issue: gh:149\n  title: 'Landed directly (2026-09-04), not by a delegated worker: full Float for jq -- lane had 4.3h-idle real progress (arithmetic, comparisons, input, a careful ESCALATION.md) stalled by the worker''s own model repeatedly corrupting a probe script with a stray Unicode combining character. Closed the escalation''s printing gap: jq''s tostring already gets the shortest round-trip digits right (verified against a ~5000-value fuzz run, matching float-build-c-escalation''s own verification), so its output is reparsed and relaid-out with the same notation rule runtime/toylang.c''s Native formatter uses, rather than needing a fresh Q37 decision round. One gap left open on purpose: a non-finite float nested in a Vec/Record diverges from JS (pinned by a test, not silently untested) -- fixing it means jq abandoning its value-based JSON encoder for hand-built string concatenation, a bigger change than this row''s scope.'\n  kind: build\n  needs: [float-build]\n  status: done\n\n- id: float-build-c-escalation\n  issue: gh:149\n  title: 'Landed directly (2026-09-04), not by a delegated worker: full Float support for Native/LLVM, not an escalation -- lane had sat delegated 23.5 hours, 0 commits, last run burned its whole budget re-deriving history before a blocked CLI call ended it. libc has no shortest-round-trip float formatter (the actual \"usable native matching primitive\" question), so one was built: increasing-precision search over %e for the digits, ECMA-262 Number::toString layout for the notation, verified against Node byte-for-byte across a ~5000-value fuzz run before landing in runtime/toylang.c. Also fixed a real Kind-mismatch bug in check/mod.rs''s `-x` desugaring (Float-typed zero built as Kind::Int(0)), general to every backend, that JS''s untyped runtime numbers were masking. Float still needs Go/Python/Rust/Lua before it can be a corpus case.'\n  kind: build\n  needs: [float-build]\n  status: done\n\n- id: benchmark-suite-build\n  title: 'Landed directly (2026-09-03), not by a delegated worker: the hyperfine harness (src/bin/bench.rs, just bench NAME) and binary-trees, the first of the 8 non-float CLBG tasks, per benchmark-plan.md''s Build status section. That section also corrects the suite spike''s \"good fit today\" claim: reverse-complement/k-nucleotide/regex-redux need Str slicing or Char -> Str, neither of which exists; pidigits needs a bignum type; mandelbrot needs float support most backends lack. fasta and fannkuch-redux are the two next real candidates (benchmark-fasta-build, benchmark-fannkuch-redux-build below); n-body/spectral-norm stay deferred (gh:146 ruling).'\n  kind: build\n  needs: []\n  prio: 4\n  status: done","truncated":true,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-benchmark-fannkuch-redux-build/plans/board.yaml","text":"- id: float-build-jq\n  issue: gh:149\n  title: 'Landed directly (2026-09-04), not by a delegated worker: full Float for jq -- lane had 4.3h-idle real progress (arithmetic, comparisons, input, a careful ESCALATION.md) stalled by the worker''s own model repeatedly corrupting a probe script with a stray Unicode combining character. Closed the escalation''s printing gap: jq''s tostring already gets the shortest round-trip digits right (verified against a ~5000-value fuzz run, matching float-build-c-escalation''s own verification), so its output is reparsed and relaid-out with the same notation rule runtime/toylang.c''s Native formatter uses, rather than needing a fresh Q37 decision round. One gap left open on purpose: a non-finite float nested in a Vec/Record diverges from JS (pinned by a test, not silently untested) -- fixing it means jq abandoning its value-based JSON encoder for hand-built string concatenation, a bigger change than this row''s scope.'\n  kind: build\n  needs: [float-build]\n  status: done\n\n- id: float-build-c-escalation\n  issue: gh:149\n  title: 'Landed directly (2026-09-04), not by a delegated worker: full Float support for Native/LLVM, not an escalation -- lane had sat delegated 23.5 hours, 0 commits, last run burned its whole budget re-deriving history before a blocked CLI call ended it. libc has no shortest-round-trip float formatter (the actual \"usable native matching primitive\" question), so one was built: increasing-precision search over %e for the digits, ECMA-262 Number::toString layout for the notation, verified against Node byte-for-byte across a ~5000-value fuzz run before landing in runtime/toylang.c. Also fixed a real Kind-mismatch bug in check/mod.rs''s `-x` desugaring (Float-typed zero built as Kind::Int(0)), general to every backend, that JS''s untyped runtime numbers were masking. Float still needs Go/Python/Rust/Lua before it can be a corpus case.'\n  kind: build\n  needs: [float-build]\n  status: done\n\n- id: benchmark-suite-build\n  title: 'Landed directly (2026-09-03), not by a delegated worker: the hyperfine harness (src/bin/bench.rs, just bench NAME) and binary-trees, the first of the 8 non-float CLBG tasks, per benchmark-plan.md''s Build status section. That section also corrects the suite spike''s \"good fit today\" claim: reverse-complement/k-nucleotide/regex-redux need Str slicing or Char -> Str, neither of which exists; pidigits needs a bignum type; mandelbrot needs float support most backends lack. fasta and fannkuch-redux are the two next real candidates (benchmark-fasta-build, benchmark-fannkuch-redux-build below); n-body/spectral-norm stay deferred (gh:146 ruling).'\n  kind: build\n  needs: []\n  prio: 4\n  status: done\n\n- id: benchmark-fasta-build\n  title: 'Build the CLBG fasta task in toylang (benchmark-plan.md Build status): generates DNA sequences from a small literal Str alphabet via a computed PRNG index, so it needs no Char -> Str conversion. Follows the pattern in benches/programs/binary-trees.toy -- program under benches/programs/, correctness pinned as a small-N tests/corpus/ case, a larger benches/inputs/ fixture for real timing.'\n  kind: build\n  needs: []\n  prio: 4\n  status: todo\n\n- id: benchmark-fannkuch-redux-build\n  title: 'Build the CLBG fannkuch-redux task in toylang (benchmark-plan.md Build status): permutations of Vec<Int>, fully numeric, no string decode needed. Follows the pattern in benches/programs/binary-trees.toy.'\n  kind: build\n  needs: []\n  prio: 4\n  status: todo\n\n- id: search-layer-build\n  title: Wire first/any/all into the general nondeterministic-evaluation model from draft.md#query-is-search (gh:142 ruling, coordinate with search-and-fold-design)\n  kind: build\n  needs: []\n  status: delegated\n\n- id: sort-by-max-by\n  issue: gh:177\n  title: sort_by and max_by -- ordering through a scalar projection (euler-ergonomics row 7)\n  kind: build\n  needs: [sum-max-builtins-build]\n  status: delegated\n\n- id: param-destructure-build\n  title: Implement match-arm-style parameter destructuring, e.g. fn gcd({a, b}:{a:Int,b:Int}) (gh:144 ruling)","lineStart":160,"lineEnd":209,"totalLines":488,"truncated":true}},"title":"plans/board.yaml","time":{"start":1788686255766,"end":1788686255779}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"The issue reference is `benchmark-fannkuch-redux-build`. Let me find the actual issue number. The board row id is `benchmark-fannkuch-redux-build`. Let me check the board.yaml for the issue number. Actually the brief says run `gh issue view benchmark-fannkuch-redux-build`. That failed. Let me look for the issue number. The board might reference an issue number.\n\nLet me look at board.yaml around line 188.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_0760245e80018cf1j6W0ewR0M5","sessionID":"ses_f89fe1261ffe8viTNFDooEJYor","messageID":"msg_076020e0d001LhiKERM5q99gAP"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788686256557,"sessionID":"ses_f89fe1261ffe8viTNFDooEJYor","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-b1b288a232fb3452","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-benchmark-fannkuch-redux-build/plans/benchmark-plan.md","offset":95,"limit":50},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-benchmark-fannkuch-redux-build/plans/benchmark-plan.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n95: recursive count doesn't already exercise, so it was left out rather than force-fit.\n96: \n97: **The suite spike's \"good fit today\" claim for fasta/k-nucleotide/reverse-complement/regex-redux\n98: does not hold against the current builtin set.** `chars(s)` decodes `Str` to `Vec<Char>`, but\n99: `Char` has no wire form and there is no `Char -> Str` builtin and no `Str` slice/index operator\n100: (`docs/reference/types/char.md`; confirmed empirically, not just read off the docs, since the\n101: docs could themselves be stale) -- so a program cannot decode an existing string, transform it\n102: character by character, and print the result. That blocks reverse-complement (decode, complement,\n103: re-encode) and k-nucleotide (decode into k-length windows, print each as a string) outright.\n104: regex-redux additionally has no regex engine to build on. fasta is different: it only *generates*\n105: text from a small fixed alphabet of literal `Str` values chosen by a computed index, never\n106: decodes anything, so it stays buildable -- just not yet built. pidigits needs digits of pi beyond\n107: what a 32-bit or even 64-bit accumulator holds; the spigot algorithm's usual unbounded-bignum\n108: shape has no home in a language with no bignum type, so it is blocked the same way the float\n109: tasks are, on a type the language does not have yet.\n110: \n111: Of the eight, `binary-trees` is landed; `fasta` and `fannkuch-redux` (both fully numeric or\n112: literal-driven, no decode needed) are the next real candidates; `mandelbrot` needs float support\n113: most backends don't have yet, the same gate n-body/spectral-norm are already behind; `pidigits`\n114: needs a bignum type; `reverse-complement`, `k-nucleotide`, and `regex-redux` need `Str`\n115: slicing or a `Char -> Str` builtin, neither of which exists.\n116: \n117: ## What this plan leaves to the next person\n118: \n119: - Whether n-body and spectral-norm stay in the initial set, and whether float semantics (the\n120:   `q37-float-semantics` / `float-build` board rows, both still `todo`) gate them.\n121: - A dashboard, when there is a baseline and a specific regression it would have caught.\n122: \n123: These are named rather than resolved here because the spikes do not settle them.\n\n(End of file - total 123 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"recursive count doesn't already exercise, so it was left out rather than force-fit.\n\n**The suite spike's \"good fit today\" claim for fasta/k-nucleotide/reverse-complement/regex-redux\ndoes not hold against the current builtin set.** `chars(s)` decodes `Str` to `Vec<Char>`, but\n`Char` has no wire form and there is no `Char -> Str` builtin and no `Str` slice/index operator\n(`docs/reference/types/char.md`; confirmed empirically, not just read off the docs, since the\ndocs could themselves be stale) -- so a program cannot decode an existing string, transform it\ncharacter by character, and print the result. That blocks reverse-complement (decode, complement,\nre-encode) and k-nucleotide (decode into k-length windows, print each as a string) outright.\nregex-redux additionally has no regex engine to build on. fasta is different: it only *generates*\ntext from a small fixed alphabet of literal `Str` values chosen by a computed index, never\ndecodes anything, so it stays buildable -- just not yet built. pidigits needs digits of pi beyond\nwhat a 32-bit or even 64-bit accumulator holds; the spigot algorithm's usual unbounded-bignum\nshape has no home in a language with no bignum type, so it is blocked the same way the float\ntasks are, on a type the language does not have yet.\n\nOf the eight, `binary-trees` is landed; `fasta` and `fannkuch-redux` (both fully numeric or\nliteral-driven, no decode needed) are the next real candidates; `mandelbrot` needs float support\nmost backends don't have yet, the same gate n-body/spectral-norm are already behind; `pidigits`\nneeds a bignum type; `reverse-complement`, `k-nucleotide`, and `regex-redux` need `Str`","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-benchmark-fannkuch-redux-build/plans/benchmark-plan.md","text":"recursive count doesn't already exercise, so it was left out rather than force-fit.\n\n**The suite spike's \"good fit today\" claim for fasta/k-nucleotide/reverse-complement/regex-redux\ndoes not hold against the current builtin set.** `chars(s)` decodes `Str` to `Vec<Char>`, but\n`Char` has no wire form and there is no `Char -> Str` builtin and no `Str` slice/index operator\n(`docs/reference/types/char.md`; confirmed empirically, not just read off the docs, since the\ndocs could themselves be stale) -- so a program cannot decode an existing string, transform it\ncharacter by character, and print the result. That blocks reverse-complement (decode, complement,\nre-encode) and k-nucleotide (decode into k-length windows, print each as a string) outright.\nregex-redux additionally has no regex engine to build on. fasta is different: it only *generates*\ntext from a small fixed alphabet of literal `Str` values chosen by a computed index, never\ndecodes anything, so it stays buildable -- just not yet built. pidigits needs digits of pi beyond\nwhat a 32-bit or even 64-bit accumulator holds; the spigot algorithm's usual unbounded-bignum\nshape has no home in a language with no bignum type, so it is blocked the same way the float\ntasks are, on a type the language does not have yet.\n\nOf the eight, `binary-trees` is landed; `fasta` and `fannkuch-redux` (both fully numeric or\nliteral-driven, no decode needed) are the next real candidates; `mandelbrot` needs float support\nmost backends don't have yet, the same gate n-body/spectral-norm are already behind; `pidigits`\nneeds a bignum type; `reverse-complement`, `k-nucleotide`, and `regex-redux` need `Str`\nslicing or a `Char -> Str` builtin, neither of which exists.\n\n## What this plan leaves to the next person\n\n- Whether n-body and spectral-norm stay in the initial set, and whether float semantics (the\n  `q37-float-semantics` / `float-build` board rows, both still `todo`) gate them.\n- A dashboard, when there is a baseline and a specific regression it would have caught.\n\nThese are named rather than resolved here because the spikes do not settle them.","lineStart":95,"lineEnd":123,"totalLines":123,"truncated":false}},"title":"plans/benchmark-plan.md","time":{"start":1788686256546,"end":1788686256554}},"id":"prt_07602469c001KvcDOoDI2skMSI","sessionID":"ses_f89fe1261ffe8viTNFDooEJYor","messageID":"msg_076020e0d001LhiKERM5q99gAP"}}
