{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788686314370,"sessionID":"ses_f89fe1261ffe8viTNFDooEJYor","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"write","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-9849e6edeebe1cf1","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-benchmark-fannkuch-redux-build/ref_fannkuch.py","content":"def fannkuch(n):\n    perm1 = list(range(n))\n    count = [0] * n\n    max_flips = 0\n    checksum = 0\n    perm = [0] * n\n    r = n\n    while True:\n        while r != 1:\n            count[r - 1] = r\n            r -= 1\n        for i in range(n):\n            perm[i] = perm1[i]\n        flips = 0\n        k = perm[0]\n        while k != 0:\n            k2 = (k + 1) // 2\n            for i in range(k2):\n                t = perm[i]\n                perm[i] = perm[k - i]\n                perm[k - i] = t\n            flips += 1\n            k = perm[0]\n        if flips > max_flips:\n            max_flips = flips\n        checksum += flips if flips % 2 == 0 else -flips\n        while True:\n            if r == n:\n                return checksum, max_flips\n            perm0 = perm1[0]\n            i = 0\n            while i < r:\n                j = i + 1\n                perm1[i] = perm1[j]\n                i = j\n            perm1[r] = perm0\n            count[r] -= 1\n            if count[r] > 0:\n                break\n            r += 1\n\n\nfor n in (6, 7, 8):\n    print(n, fannkuch(n))\n"},"output":"Wrote file successfully.","metadata":{"diagnostics":{},"filepath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-benchmark-fannkuch-redux-build/ref_fannkuch.py","exists":false,"truncated":false},"title":"ref_fannkuch.py","time":{"start":1788686314363,"end":1788686314369}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Now let me understand the CLBG fannkuch-redux task. The fannkuch-redux algorithm:\n\nGiven N, compute fannkuch for permutations of size N. The classic CLBG task:\n\n```\n/* The Computer Language Benchmarks Game\n   https://salsa.debian.org/benchmarksgame-team/benchmarksgame/\n   contributed by Isaac Gouy, transliterated by Oleg Mazurov\n*/\n\n#include <stdio.h>\n#include <stdlib.h>\n#include <string.h>\n\nint fannkuch(int n) {\n   int *perm1 = malloc(n * sizeof(int));\n   int *perm = malloc(n * sizeof(int));\n   int *count = malloc(n * sizeof(int));\n   int maxFlipsCount = 0;\n   int permCount = 0;\n   int checksum = 0;\n\n   for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) perm1[i] = i;\n   int r = n;\n\n   for (;;) {\n      while (r != 1) { count[r-1] = r; r--; }\n      for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) perm[i] = perm1[i];\n      int flipsCount = 0;\n      int k;\n\n      while (!((k = perm[0]) == 0)) {\n         int k2 = (k+1) >> 1;\n         for (int i = 0; i < k2; i++) {\n            int temp = perm[i];\n            perm[i] = perm[k-i];\n            perm[k-i] = temp;\n         }\n         flipsCount++;\n      }\n\n      if (flipsCount > maxFlipsCount) maxFlipsCount = flipsCount;\n      checksum += flipsCount % 2 == 0 ? flipsCount : -flipsCount;\n\n      // generate next permutation\n      for (;;) {\n         if (r == n) return checksum;  // actually returns maxFlipsCount in modern versions\n         int perm0 = perm1[0];\n         int i = 0;\n         while (i < r) {\n            int j = i + 1;\n            perm1[i] = perm1[j];\n            i = j;\n         }\n         perm1[r] = perm0;\n         count[r] = count[r] - 1;\n         if (count[r] > 0) break;\n         r++;\n      }\n   }\n}\n```\n\nThe modern CLBG fannkuch-redux outputs the checksum and maxFlips:\n\n```\nint main(int argc, char *argv[]) {\n    int n = argc > 1 ? atoi(argv[1]) : 7;\n    printf(\"%d\\nPfannkuchen(%d) = %d\\n\", fannkuch(n), n, maxflips);\n}\n```\n\nWait, actually the CLBG output format is:\n```\n30\nPfannkuchen(7) = 16\n```\n\nThe checksum line then \"Pfannkuchen(N) = max\" line.\n\nLet me recall the exact output. For n=7, checksum = 228, maxflips = 16. Output:\n```\n228\nPfannkuchen(7) = 16\n```\n\nFor n=6, checksum = -66, maxflips = 7. Let me verify. Actually let me not guess - I need to be careful about the output values.\n\nThe CLBG fannkuch-redux task signature:\n```\nfannkuch-redux\nThe Computer Language Benchmarks Game\nhttps://benchmarksgame-team.pages.debian.net/benchmarksgame/description/fannkuchredux.html\n```\n\nInput: N (number of items to permute).\nOutput: two lines:\n1. checksum (alternating sign flips count sum)\n2. `Pfannkuchen(N) = max_flips`\n\nReference output for N=7:\n```\n228\nPfannkuch(7) = 16\n```\n\nWait the line is \"Pfannkuchen\" with the task name. Let me check. The original CLBG output line is:\n```\nPfannkuchen(7) = 16\n```\n\nActually the description says the output format:\n```\nN\nPfannkuchen(N) = max_flips\n```\n\nHmm, the task name is \"fannkuch-redux\" but the output line historically says \"Pfannkuchen\". Let me be careful. Actually looking at the CLBG, the output is:\n\n```\n228\nPfannkuchen(7) = 16\n```\n\nLet me verify by computing. Let me write a quick python script to compute fannkuch for small n to get exact expected outputs. But I need to be careful about the algorithm. Let me write the reference in python.\n\nActually, the task is a \"delegated worker\" — I need to implement this in toylang and pin correctness via corpus case. Let me compute the correct values.\n\nLet me implement the standard fannkuch-redux in python and compute checksum + maxflips for n=6 and n=7.\n\nLet me write a python script.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_0760316e7001N3mZprCmRdXhcl","sessionID":"ses_f89fe1261ffe8viTNFDooEJYor","messageID":"msg_07602c9a3001YidbAl7zlwO8Qn"}}
