m1[0]=0. i=0.\n    - while i<r(1): j=i+1=1; perm1[0]=perm1[1]=1; i=1. loop ends.\n  - perm1[1]=perm0=0. So perm1=[1,0,2].\n  - count[1]=count[1]-1=2-1=1. >0, break.\n- Back to top. while r!=1: r=1, skip.\n- perm=perm1=[1,0,2]. k=1. flips: k2=(1+1)/2=1. swap perm[0],perm[1] -> [0,1,2]. flips=1. k=perm[0]=0, stop.\n- checksum += -1 (odd). \n- generate next: r=1. perm0=perm1[0]=1. i=0. while i<1: j=1; perm1[0]=perm1[1]=0; i=1. perm1[1]=1. perm1=[0,1,2]. count[1]-=1=0. not >0, so r++ -> r=2.\n  - loop again: r=2 != n=3. perm0=perm1[0]=0. i=0. while i<2: j=1; perm1[0]=perm1[1]=1; i=1; j=2; perm1[1]=perm1[2]=2; i=2 stop. perm1[2]=perm0=0. perm1=[1,2,0]. count[2]-=1=3-1=2 >0 break.\n- perm=perm1=[1,2,0]. k=1. k2=1 swap perm[0],perm[1]: [2,1,0]. flips=1. k=perm[0]=2. k2=(2+1)/2=1. swap perm[0],perm[2]: [0,1,2]. flips=2. k=0. checksum += +2 (even).\n- ... etc.\n\nThis looks standard. So my python is faithful to the standard algorithm. So the discrepancy with 228 suggests the known value isn't from this exact algorithm, OR my memory of 228 is wrong.\n\nLet me actually verify with the real CLBG. Let me fetch the reference output. The CLBG site test data... Let me just check a well-known Go implementation output. Actually, let me reconsider - maybe the CLBG answer for N=7 IS 228 and the difference is that the algorithm includes counting differently.\n\nHmm, let me reconsider. Actually I recall that the CLBG fannkuch-redux checksum for N=7 might indeed be 228. Let me double check by computing the total with a different well-known implementation.\n\nActually, let me look at the real CLBG reference implementation to make sure my algorithm matches it exactly. Let me fetch it.\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider the permutation generation. In my code:\n\n```\nwhile True:\n    if r == n: return\n    perm0 = perm1[0]\n    i = 0\n    while i < r:\n        j = i + 1\n        perm1[i] = perm1[j]\n        i = j\n    perm1[r] = perm0\n    count[r] -= 1\n    if count[r] > 0: break\n    r +